hi there steve kaufman here  i'm back from montreal  i uh attended a conference there  called an international conference on  multilingualism linguistic challenges  and neurocognitive mechanisms  and  i attended the conference  in the hope that it would provide me  with some insights on language learning  which i could use for my own language  learning which we could introduce  perhaps at link  and  i will post by the way  a link to the conference  here  because they are going to put up  i gather  full  videos of all of the presentations so uh  you can all  participate unless that's somehow  limited to people who were signed up i  don't know but  um i will post a link  um  my conclusions um i didn't learn  much really that is relevant to language  learning  i had a wonderful time i enjoyed being  in montreal  montreal is really amazingly bilingual  when you you see people in the streets  switching back and forth from english to  french if you're in a restaurant or  a bar you have no idea if the person  serving you is english anglophone or  francophone because they seem to speak  both languages so freely  so it's quite a unique experience  and i like downtown montreal around  mcgill university and  and uh you know the different streets  there  um  the uh concert the conference was  sponsored by the school of communication  sciences and disorders  disorders of mcgill university  the people  the linguistics and and brain i guess  the neuro  scientists and people they were  extremely nice and friendly and  enthusiastic  and so it was a wonderful two days  the um  the uh and it was a whole new field for  me i spent the first day not  understanding a lot of it because they  were talking about  n400 and p600 you know what n400 is  it's a reaction in your brain after 400  milliseconds when you discover something  that uh  maybe shouldn't be there or whatever and  then and i'm not sure the difference  between an n400 and a p600 but these are  things that  these people measure  uh and a lot of the uh i should have  that there were like presentations every  half hour there are a lot of  presentations in addition to which they  had these poster sessions where people  would uh put up posters describing the  type of research they've been doing  and and you could interact with them and  ask them questions and so forth so  there's a massive amount of information  most of it having to do with very  specific and very sort of detailed  studies of some aspect of  of near  neurocognitive mechanisms and so they  measure  how the brain  reacts to  certain stimulus which could be  something that's correct or incorrect in  speech and so forth  so it went into a lot of this kind of  detail  i mean i read here morphological  processing in late bilinguals the role  of language experience in the neural  cognition of late learned language  bilingual l3a versus monolingual l2a  cross-linguistic syntactic priming in  german english bilinguals the role of  global and surface syntactic structure  and and it just goes on and on like this  and  you know i guess i'm still trying to  process  this whole new world  for me  but uh i was not the only one who  was there who was not from this specific  background  and who felt it wasn't very relevant to  language learning  so it's a different world  i have to bear in mind that the school  of communication sciences and disorders  a large part of their purpose is to  train  speech pathologists and people like that  who help those people who have you know  difficulties auditory difficulties  speech difficulties and so forth and so  it's almost as if  language learners were treated as some  kind of a  you know pathological case and so they  measured how the brain reacts and so  forth and so on  um  it was interesting in talking to people  because it was very easy to talk to  people everybody was happy to talk to  you about what they were doing about or  about anything in general  and  the attitude about language learning or  the relevance of this to language  learning  i would say that it varied from one  person who felt that if  language learners were taught  linguistics that they would learn  languages faster this was very much a  minority view a majority of people  actually believed that and i think it  also came through in the presentations  that ultimately language acquisition  depends on exposure  which is not earth shouting earth  shattering really  and  and many people confirmed that there was  this dichotomy between the  kind of research that is done  and then the needs of the language  acquisition  community although  theoretically  you know these people many of them were  involved in both areas so for me it was  very exciting to be introduced to a new  sort of discipline  and i'm truly still sorting out what the  relevance of this is  to uh language learning and i'll  probably watch the videos again because  first time through there's a lot that i  didn't quite understand  uh so that was the um  one thing by the way as i was i took a  taxi in montreal and the taxi driver was  italian which i could tell by his accent  in french so then we switched to italian  he spoke french very fluently but he  spoke with a very heavy italian accent  he'd been living in montreal for 35  years i think he was about maybe in his  mid 50s  so um  what does that say  it just says that  you can be exposed to the language and  and develop a high degree of fluency  and yet  not change your accent at all  and yet you can have someone who within  a year or two  uh you have like lucca from rome  who uh  you know speaks french like a native is  that talent is that  well is that  you know depends on i guess your goals  your motivation  i mean i uh i came away i guess  more confirmed than ever in in the  belief that uh  language learning is all about what you  want to do it's motivation  motivation spending the time and  and as i say this this ability to notice  which can be helped by  studying grammar or interacting with a  teacher but but that's a small part and  that the big part is the motivation and  the amount of time exposure to the  language uh  and so details of what you do and so  forth don't matter  and it doesn't matter how old you are  this is something that came through in  the conference the sort of increasing  awareness of the importance of  of this  neuroplastic plasticity that we have  a number of people also confirmed that  these ideas of of a universal grammar  are  are basically fading out of favor  linguistics becomes more of a science of  observation experimentation and looking  at how the brain actually reacts to  different stimuli different uh exposure  to different forms of the language or  different languages  rather than a linguistics that was based  on some logical extrapolation a  lachansky's universal grammar  although the defenders of the universal  grammar continue to support it and claim  that it's constantly  evolving  however  the fundamental tenet of universal  grammar is that there is insufficient  stimulus you know  for a child to simply start speaking  after two or three years  which suggests  at least to chomsky that therefore there  must be some inherent  ability to to  identify or to create language based on  certain inherent grammatical rules  whereas i think  increasingly with this experimentation  and so forth at least a number of people  that i spoke to agreed with me that it's  basically the brain responding to  stimulus the way it responds to other  stimulus and forming rules and patterns  to deal with it in the brain  uh  but that's about as far as i'm gonna  venture because i'm not  really qualified to say much more so  a pleasant experience i'm not sure what  it does for language learning or for my  understanding of language learning  but i enjoy myself so thank you for  listening bye for now
 

